        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-on" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why ON Gets Its Own Guide</a>
          <a href="#how-we-matched" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Matched Tubs to Goals</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#lineup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Lineup at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#whey-isolate-hydro" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Gold Standard vs Isolate vs Hydrowhey</a>
          <a href="#casein" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. When Casein Earns a Place</a>
          <a href="#plant" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Gold Standard 100% Plant</a>
          <a href="#gainers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Serious Mass vs Pro Gainer</a>
          <a href="#testing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Testing and Label Honesty</a>
          <a href="#buying" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Buying ON Without Overpaying</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Optimum Nutrition sells enough protein powders that picking the wrong one is easy. Gold Standard 100% Whey is the default for good reason, but it is not the best ON product for everyone: the isolate suits dieters and the lactose sensitive, casein suits evenings, the plant powder suits vegans, and the two gainers suit very different people. This guide maps each of the main tubs to the goal it actually serves, so you buy the one you need rather than the one with the most famous label.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Optimum Nutrition Gets Its Own Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition is one of the oldest and most widely stocked names in sports nutrition. It has been owned by the Irish nutrition group Glanbia since 2008, and its Gold Standard 100% Whey is one of the best-known protein powders in the world, sold in supermarkets, pharmacies, gyms and every major online retailer. When people search for the best protein powder from Optimum Nutrition, they usually already trust the brand and want to know which tub to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            That question is harder than it looks because the lineup has grown well beyond the original whey. There is now a Gold Standard isolate, a hydrolyzed premium whey, a micellar casein, a plant protein, a lean gainer and a heavyweight mass gainer, plus ready-to-drink shakes, bars and pre-workouts we do not cover here. The products overlap in places and diverge sharply in others: a scoop of the isolate and a serving of Serious Mass differ by more than a thousand calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            A single-brand guide is also useful because it makes comparison fair. When everything comes from the same manufacturer, differences in protein source, scoop size and calories are design choices rather than brand quirks, which makes it easy to see exactly what each upgrade buys you. Moving from Gold Standard to the isolate, for example, trades a slightly higher price for a few grams less lactose, fat and carbohydrate per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means ON is automatically the best choice. Several competitors match or beat it on cost per 25 g of protein, certification or ingredient simplicity, and we cover them in our main <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder rankings</a> and the <a href="/protein-powder-brands/">protein powder brands guide</a>. If you have already decided on ON, though, this page will tell you which of its products fits your goal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-matched" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Matched Each ON Tub to a Goal
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab test these products or run our own taste panels. The guide is built on label analysis, ON's published product information, certification claims and the research on protein type, timing and dose, following the method on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste, texture or digestion, we are summarizing what buyers widely report rather than claiming hands-on results.
          </p>

          <p>
            For each product we recorded the protein source, protein per serving, serving weight, calories, carbohydrate and sweetener from the label at the time of writing, then worked out protein share by weight and by calorie. Those ratios separate the products cleanly. The whey and isolate products are dense and lean; casein is slightly less dense and much slower to digest; the plant protein is less dense because of its creamer and gums; and the gainers are built around carbohydrate, with protein as a minority of their calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then asked what goal each product design suits. A fast-digesting, low-lactose isolate suits cutting and sensitive stomachs. A slow-digesting casein suits a pre-bed or long-gap shake. A gainer suits someone who cannot eat enough calories, and the protein-to-carbohydrate ratio decides whether that is a lean bulk or an all-out mass phase. Price was considered within the lineup, so we flag when a premium product costs more without delivering a meaningful benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also checked that each product is currently sold on major US retail sites, since ON has retired and reformulated products over the years. The older USDA Organic plant protein, for example, has been replaced by the current Gold Standard 100% Plant formula, and we cover only the current version. Formulas and certifications change, so confirm the details on the tub you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Optimum Nutrition Protein Powders, by Goal
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the ON protein powders and gainers we found currently sold on major US retail sites at the time of writing. Each is matched to the goal it serves best rather than ranked against the others on a single scale, because a casein and a mass gainer are not competing for the same buyer. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor and region.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
