        <div id="whey-isolate-hydro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gold Standard Whey vs Gold Standard Isolate vs Platinum Hydrowhey
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three are the core of the lineup and the products people most often confuse. Gold Standard 100% Whey is a blend with whey protein isolate as its primary source, alongside whey concentrate and whey peptides. It lists about 24 g of protein for roughly 120 calories per scoop. It is the cheapest of the three per 25 g of protein in most retail listings, has the widest flavor range, and buyers consistently rate its flavors highly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gold Standard 100% Isolate drops the concentrate. ON describes it as ultra-filtered whey isolate with a portion hydrolyzed, and label listings give about 25 g of protein for roughly 100 to 110 calories, with around 1 g of carbohydrate and half a gram of fat per scoop. The upgrade buys you less lactose and slightly leaner macros. That matters for people who get bloated on concentrate and for dieters tracking every calorie; for everyone else, the original is the better value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Platinum Hydrowhey is ON's premium whey, built on hydrolyzed whey protein isolate, with about 30 g of protein for roughly 140 calories per scoop. The hydrolysis pre-digests the protein into shorter peptides, which may help some people who find regular whey hard on the stomach. It does not build more muscle than isolate at the same dose, and its bigger per-scoop number comes partly from a bigger scoop of around 40 g. Our <a href="/best-high-protein-powder/">highest protein powder guide</a> explains why that distinction matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict is simple. Buy Gold Standard 100% Whey unless you have a reason not to. Move to the isolate if lactose or calories are a real constraint. Buy Hydrowhey only if you want a 30 g dose in a single scoop, or you have tried the isolate and still find ordinary whey hard to digest, and you are comfortable paying a premium. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate comparison</a> covers the underlying trade-off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Gold Standard Casein Earns a Place
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein is the other main protein in milk, and it behaves very differently from whey. In the acidic environment of the stomach it forms a gel that digests slowly, releasing amino acids into the blood over several hours instead of the quick spike you get from whey. Gold Standard 100% Casein is built on micellar casein and lists about 24 g of protein for roughly 120 calories per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            That slow release makes casein a logical pre-bed shake. In a frequently cited 2012 study, young men who took 40 g of casein before sleep after an evening workout showed higher overnight muscle protein synthesis than those who took a placebo. Later research has broadly supported the idea that pre-sleep protein is used for muscle building, though the long-term advantage over simply hitting your daily protein target is modest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is also thick. Buyers consistently describe Gold Standard Casein as heavier in a shaker than whey, and many mix it with less liquid to make a pudding-like snack. That can be a genuine advantage for dieters whose evenings are the hardest part of the day, because a bowl of casein pudding is filling and low in calories. It is a disadvantage if you want a quick, thin shake after training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people do not need both whey and casein. If you take one shake a day, take whichever fits your routine and preferences. Casein earns a place alongside whey mainly for people who train in the evening, who go long stretches without eating, or who want a filling dessert substitute. Our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein protein rankings</a> compare Gold Standard Casein with other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gold Standard 100% Plant: How It Compares to Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            ON's current plant protein combines pea protein, fava bean protein isolate and brown rice protein. ON lists 24 g of protein per serving with all nine essential amino acids, 0 g of sugar, and no artificial colors, flavors or sweeteners; it is sweetened with stevia. A retailer label we found shows about 150 calories in a serving of roughly 37 g, so it is a little less dense than the whey products because of its creamer and gums.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blending pea with rice is a sensible design. Pea protein is relatively low in methionine and rice is relatively low in lysine, so the two complement each other. Even so, plant blends generally carry somewhat less leucine per gram than whey, so a slightly larger serving narrows the gap if muscle building is your priority. Research comparing pea or blended plant protein with whey at adequate doses alongside resistance training has generally found similar results.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger consideration with any plant protein is contaminants. Plants absorb minerals from soil, and independent testing such as Consumer Reports' October 2025 report has found higher average lead levels in plant-based protein products than in dairy-based ones. We have not seen published heavy metal results for this specific product, so if that matters to you, ask ON for testing information or choose a plant protein that publishes its results. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to read those reports.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gold Standard 100% Plant is a reasonable choice if you want a vegan powder from a brand you already know and you prefer stevia to sucralose. If you are dairy-free and price-sensitive, a single-ingredient pea isolate will usually cost less per 25 g. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein rankings</a> and the <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein guide</a> cover alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gainers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Serious Mass vs Pro Gainer: Which Gainer, If Any
          </h2>

          <p>
            ON's two gainers are often lumped together, but they are built for different people. Serious Mass delivers about 1,250 calories and 50 g of protein per full serving mixed with water, per the label, and most of its calories come from maltodextrin. That means only about 16 percent of its energy comes from protein. It is designed for people who genuinely struggle to eat enough food to gain weight, and buyers frequently recommend starting with half a serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pro Gainer is closer to a high-protein meal. It lists about 60 g of protein in roughly 650 calories per full serving, which means around 37 percent of its calories come from protein, more than double the share in Serious Mass. That makes it easier to fit into a lean bulk, where you want a moderate calorie surplus and a high protein intake without a huge carbohydrate load. Many buyers also find it easier to digest than the 1,250-calorie gainers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before buying either, consider whether you need a gainer at all. A shake of Gold Standard whey blended with whole milk, oats, a banana and peanut butter delivers a large dose of calories and protein from foods you control, often at a lower cost per calorie. Gainers win on convenience and on volume: a liquid serving is easier to finish than the equivalent food when appetite is the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: if you are a naturally lean person who cannot eat enough to grow, Serious Mass is a blunt but effective tool; start with a half serving. If you want a convenient high-calorie shake during a controlled bulk, Pro Gainer is the better design. If you can eat more food, do that first. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight gain guide</a> compares gainers with DIY shakes in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Testing, Certification and Label Honesty at ON
          </h2>

          <p>
            One reason ON is often the default recommendation is that its core whey has a long record of independent testing. Gold Standard 100% Whey is widely promoted as Informed Choice certified, a program that screens products for substances banned in sport, and ON describes several other products, including the isolate and the plant protein, as banned-substance tested. Certification is product and flavor specific, so confirm the exact product in the certifier's database before relying on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete in a tested sport, check whether the specific product carries Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport, the batch-tested programs most sports bodies recognize. A general banned-substance testing claim on a retailer listing is not the same as batch-level certification, and the safe approach is to verify on the certifier's website rather than trust the packaging alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            On label honesty, the ON products in this guide disclose their protein sources and do not list free amino acids among them in a way that suggests amino spiking. Gold Standard lists its whey forms in order of weight, and the isolate and Hydrowhey use whey isolate throughout. The flavored whey products use sucralose and some contain soy lecithin, which matters if you avoid artificial sweeteners or soy; the plant protein uses stevia.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a separate question from banned substances. Dairy proteins generally test lower than plant proteins for lead, and no protein powder is completely free of trace metals. If you want published heavy metal results, ask ON directly or consider a brand that posts lab results online. Our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder guide</a> lists products with verifiable testing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Buy Optimum Nutrition Without Overpaying
          </h2>

          <p>
            ON products are among the most frequently discounted protein powders on the market, so there is rarely a reason to pay full list price. Larger tubs almost always cost less per 25 g of protein than smaller ones, and Gold Standard whey in the five-pound size is the product most often on sale. Compare cost per 25 g of protein rather than tub price, because scoop sizes and serving counts differ across the lineup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy from the brand's own storefront or from listings sold directly by a major retailer. Popular supplements are a target for counterfeiting and for third-party sellers offering old stock, and a deal that looks too good from an unknown marketplace seller is not worth the risk. Check the seal, the lot number and the best-by date when the tub arrives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try a small size of any flavor you have not had before. ON's flavor range is wide and buyer opinions differ, and the savings of a five-pound tub disappear if you cannot finish it. Double Rich Chocolate is the Gold Standard flavor most retail listings lead with, but taste is personal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that ON is not always the cheapest way to get good protein. If cost per 25 g is your main concern, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder guide</a> ranks budget options, some of which undercut Gold Standard while keeping a clean label. If you value ON's availability, testing record and flavors, though, Gold Standard 100% Whey remains a sensible place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

